WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:04.182
We've already seen many
examples of infinite series.

00:04.182 --> 00:06.765
But what's exciting about what
we're about to do in this video

00:06.765 --> 00:09.879
is we're going to use infinite
series to define a function.

00:09.879 --> 00:11.420
And the most common
one that you will

00:11.420 --> 00:17.120
see in your mathematical
careers is the power series.

00:17.120 --> 00:20.010
And I'm about to write a general
case of the power series.

00:20.010 --> 00:22.250
So I could imagine
a function, f of x,

00:22.250 --> 00:26.100
being defined as
the infinite sum.

00:26.100 --> 00:30.900
So going from n equals 0
to infinity of a sub n--

00:30.900 --> 00:33.760
so a sub n is just going to be
the coefficient on each term--

00:33.760 --> 00:37.320
times our variable x
minus some constant c.

00:37.320 --> 00:39.160
You could almost
imagine this is shifting

00:39.160 --> 00:42.260
our function to the n-th power.

00:42.260 --> 00:43.910
So if I were to
expand this out, I

00:43.910 --> 00:46.390
have my first term's
coefficient, a sub 0,

00:46.390 --> 00:50.630
times x minus c
to the 0-th power,

00:50.630 --> 00:54.400
plus a sub 1 times x minus
c to the first power.

00:54.400 --> 00:57.180
This one, of course, will
simplify just a sub 0.

00:57.180 --> 01:01.590
This would simplify to a sub
1 times x minus c plus a sub

01:01.590 --> 01:04.700
2 times x minus c squared.

01:04.700 --> 01:07.587
And I could just keep
going on and on and on.

01:07.587 --> 01:09.170
Now, when you see
this, you might say,

01:09.170 --> 01:10.760
aren't our geometric
series, don't

01:10.760 --> 01:13.440
those look like a special
case of a power series

01:13.440 --> 01:17.260
if our common ratio was an x
instead of an r in that case,

01:17.260 --> 01:21.920
or if our common ratio was a
variable, I guess I could say?

01:21.920 --> 01:23.060
And you would be right.

01:23.060 --> 01:25.490
That absolutely
would be the case.

01:25.490 --> 01:28.800
So a geometric series.

01:28.800 --> 01:31.050
So let's just think about
defining a function in terms

01:31.050 --> 01:32.482
of a geometric series.

01:32.482 --> 01:33.940
And of course, we
don't have to use

01:33.940 --> 01:36.650
x all the time as the
independent variable,

01:36.650 --> 01:38.650
but this is kind of the
most typical convention.

01:38.650 --> 01:40.900
I guess we could also use r
as an independent variable

01:40.900 --> 01:42.240
if we wanted as well.

01:42.240 --> 01:44.067
But let's imagine
a function g of x.

01:44.067 --> 01:45.900
We could have g of r
if we wanted, but let's

01:45.900 --> 01:50.200
do g of x is equal
to the sum from n

01:50.200 --> 01:57.060
equals 0 to infinity
of a times x to the n.

01:57.060 --> 02:01.220
So this is kind of a typical
geometric series here.

02:01.220 --> 02:04.020
And what's the difference
between this and this?

02:04.020 --> 02:06.259
Well, the difference is is
here, for every term we're

02:06.259 --> 02:07.800
going to have the
same coefficient a,

02:07.800 --> 02:09.864
while over here we have a sub n.

02:09.864 --> 02:12.280
We're multiplying by a different
thing every time up here.

02:12.280 --> 02:14.610
We're multiplying by the
same thing over here.

02:14.610 --> 02:16.930
And in this case, this
particular geometric series

02:16.930 --> 02:19.420
I just made, instead of
having x minus c to the n,

02:19.420 --> 02:20.580
we have just x to the n.

02:20.580 --> 02:22.705
So you could say, well,
this is a special case when

02:22.705 --> 02:24.270
c is equal to 0.

02:24.270 --> 02:25.440
And we can expand it out.

02:25.440 --> 02:28.040
This is a times
x to the 0, which

02:28.040 --> 02:31.540
is just going to be a, plus
a times x to the first,

02:31.540 --> 02:33.330
plus a times x squared.

02:33.330 --> 02:36.210
And we just go on and
on and on forever.

02:36.210 --> 02:38.540
Now, what's exciting
about this is

02:38.540 --> 02:43.510
we know that this, under
certain conditions,

02:43.510 --> 02:45.950
will actually give
us a finite value.

02:45.950 --> 02:47.640
This will actually converge.

02:47.640 --> 02:51.350
This will actually, I guess,
give us a sensical answer.

02:51.350 --> 02:53.520
So under what conditions
does that happen?

02:53.520 --> 02:56.279
Well, this converges
if each of these terms

02:56.279 --> 02:57.820
gets smaller and
smaller and smaller.

02:57.820 --> 03:00.390
And each of these terms gets
smaller and smaller and smaller

03:00.390 --> 03:06.470
if the absolute value of our
common ratio is less than 1.

03:06.470 --> 03:08.100
So let me write that down.

03:08.100 --> 03:14.410
So this converges if
the absolute value

03:14.410 --> 03:18.181
of our common ratio
is less than 1.

03:18.181 --> 03:19.680
Or another way of
thinking about it,

03:19.680 --> 03:23.020
this is another way
of saying that x

03:23.020 --> 03:26.120
is in the interval
between-- it's less than 1

03:26.120 --> 03:29.280
and it is greater
than negative 1.

03:29.280 --> 03:31.640
And this term right over
here, now x is a variable.

03:31.640 --> 03:33.300
x can vary between those values.

03:33.300 --> 03:35.640
We're defining a
function in terms of x.

03:35.640 --> 03:37.740
We call this the
interval of convergence.

03:46.090 --> 03:48.487
And so we know that if
x is in this interval,

03:48.487 --> 03:50.070
this is going to
give us a finite sum.

03:50.070 --> 03:52.380
And we know what
that finite sum is.

03:52.380 --> 03:56.620
It's going to be equal
to-- if it converges.

03:56.620 --> 03:58.950
So if it converges,
this is going

03:58.950 --> 04:01.950
to be equal to our first
term, which is just

04:01.950 --> 04:05.490
a-- this simplifies to a
right over here-- over 1

04:05.490 --> 04:07.580
minus our common ratio.

04:07.580 --> 04:08.820
What's our common ratio?

04:08.820 --> 04:11.360
Our common ratio in
this example is x.

04:11.360 --> 04:15.120
Going from one term to the next,
we're just multiplying by x.

04:15.120 --> 04:19.640
We're just multiplying
by x right over there.

04:19.640 --> 04:21.510
Now, this is pretty
neat, because we're

04:21.510 --> 04:23.390
going to be able
to use this fact

04:23.390 --> 04:26.030
to put more
traditionally-defined functions

04:26.030 --> 04:28.750
into this form, and
then try to expand them

04:28.750 --> 04:31.660
out using a geometric series.

04:31.660 --> 04:33.722
And this whole idea
of using power series,

04:33.722 --> 04:35.430
or in this special
case, geometric series

04:35.430 --> 04:37.280
to represent functions,
has all sorts

04:37.280 --> 04:39.750
of applications in
engineering and finance.

04:39.750 --> 04:43.980
Using a finite number of
terms of these series,

04:43.980 --> 04:46.130
you could kind of
approximate the functions

04:46.130 --> 04:48.020
in a way that's simpler
for the human brain

04:48.020 --> 04:49.470
to understand, or
maybe a simpler

04:49.470 --> 04:51.690
way to manipulate in some way.

04:51.690 --> 04:54.610
But what's interesting
here is instead of just

04:54.610 --> 04:57.540
going from the sum
to-- instead of going

04:57.540 --> 05:01.922
from this expanded-out version
to this kind of finite value,

05:01.922 --> 05:04.130
we're now going to start
being able to take something

05:04.130 --> 05:06.930
in this form and expand it
out into a geometric series.

05:06.930 --> 05:08.734
But we have to be
careful to make sure

05:08.734 --> 05:11.150
that we're only doing it over
the interval of convergence.

05:11.150 --> 05:13.790
This is only going to be
true over the interval

05:13.790 --> 05:15.010
of convergence.

05:15.010 --> 05:18.550
Now, one other term you might
see in your mathematical career

05:18.550 --> 05:20.220
is a radius.

05:20.220 --> 05:21.945
Radius of convergence.

05:24.970 --> 05:28.770
And this is how far--
up to what value,

05:28.770 --> 05:30.180
but not including this value.

05:30.180 --> 05:36.760
So as long as our x value stays
less than a certain amount

05:36.760 --> 05:40.680
from our c value, then
this thing will converge.

05:40.680 --> 05:44.470
Now in this case,
our c value is 0.

05:44.470 --> 05:46.720
So we could ask
ourselves a question.

05:49.290 --> 05:53.120
As long as x stays
within some value of 0,

05:53.120 --> 05:54.540
this thing is going to converge.

05:54.540 --> 05:55.914
Well, you see it
right over here.

05:55.914 --> 06:01.302
As long as x stays
within one of 0.

06:01.302 --> 06:03.010
It can't go all the
way to 1, but as long

06:03.010 --> 06:05.000
as it stays less
than 1, or as long

06:05.000 --> 06:06.500
as it stays greater
than negative 1.

06:10.300 --> 06:12.490
It can stray anything
less than one

06:12.490 --> 06:14.380
away from 0, either in
the positive direction

06:14.380 --> 06:16.460
or the negative direction.

06:16.460 --> 06:18.560
Then this thing
will still converge.

06:18.560 --> 06:23.640
So we could say that our radius
of convergence is equal to 1.

06:23.640 --> 06:26.550
Another way to think about it,
our interval of convergence--

06:26.550 --> 06:28.490
we're going from
negative 1 to 1,

06:28.490 --> 06:29.980
not including those
two boundaries,

06:29.980 --> 06:31.690
so our interval is 2.

06:31.690 --> 06:34.450
So our radius of
convergence is half of that.

06:39.550 --> 06:41.920
As long as x stays
within one of 0,

06:41.920 --> 06:44.440
and that's the same thing as
saying this right over here,

06:44.440 --> 06:48.310
this series is
going to converge.
